---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const rawBase = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const base = rawBase.endsWith('/') ? rawBase : rawBase + '/';
---
<Base
  title="Advanced setup — Streamsemble"
  description="Speakers that connect themselves at boot, WLED light strips, house-wide latency, PIN pairing for TVs, and Spotify options — all environment variables."
  current="advanced"
>
  <main class="doc wrap">
    <header>
      <h1>Advanced setup</h1>
      <p class="doc__lede">
        Everything here is an environment variable on the Docker container — or
        the same key in <code>appsettings.json</code> if you run natively.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section aria-labelledby="mapping">
      <h2 id="mapping">How the settings map</h2>
      <p>
        Every setting follows one pattern: sections join with double
        underscores, and list entries get numbers. So this environment
        variable:
      </p>
      <pre is:raw><code>AirPlaySender__Targets__0__Name=Kitchen</code></pre>
      <p>is the same as this in <code>appsettings.json</code>:</p>
      <pre is:raw><code>{ "AirPlaySender": { "Targets": [ { "Name": "Kitchen" } ] } }</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="autoplay">
      <h2 id="autoplay">Speakers that connect themselves</h2>
      <p>
        Name your speakers in <code>docker-compose.yml</code> and the hub
        connects them at boot — no browser needed, ever, if you don't want one:
      </p>
      <span class="codelabel">docker-compose.yml · environment:</span>
      <pre is:raw><code>- AirPlaySender__Targets__0__Name=Kitchen
- AirPlaySender__Targets__0__Protocol=AirPlay2
- AirPlaySender__Targets__0__StreamMode=Buffered
- AirPlaySender__Targets__1__Name=Living room TV
- AirPlaySender__Targets__1__Protocol=AirPlay2
- AirPlaySender__Targets__1__StreamMode=Buffered</code></pre>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Name</strong> is the speaker's name exactly as the AirPlay
          menu shows it; the hub finds the address itself. If a device is
          stubborn about discovery, give it <code>Host</code> with an IP
          instead.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>StreamMode=Buffered</strong> is the verified mode for
          AirPlay 2 speakers — use it unless you have a reason not to.
        </li>
        <li>
          These seed the selection at boot. The web UI can still add and remove
          speakers live on top of it.
        </li>
        <li>
          If one speaker sits audibly early or late no matter what — some
          vendors misreport their own latency — nudge just that one with
          <code>LatencyTrimMs</code>, in milliseconds, positive or negative.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="wled">
      <h2 id="wled">WLED lights</h2>
      <p>
        Any WLED-flashed LED strip can light to the music. The hub times each
        light frame to land when you <em>hear</em> the beat — not when it
        decoded the audio a second or two earlier — so the flash and the drum
        hit arrive together.
      </p>
      <span class="codelabel">docker-compose.yml · environment: — one block per strip</span>
      <pre is:raw><code>- Wled__Devices__0__Name=desk
- Wled__Devices__0__Host=192.168.1.60
- Wled__Devices__0__LedCount=144
- Wled__Devices__0__Protocol=Dnrgb        # Dnrgb | Drgb | Drgbw | Warls
- Wled__Devices__0__Mode=Spectrum         # Spectrum | Vu | Pulse | Off
- Wled__Devices__0__Palette=Classic       # Classic | Solid | Rainbow
- Wled__Devices__0__Color=#FF4000
- Wled__Devices__0__Brightness=0.8        # 0–1
- Wled__Devices__0__Decay=0               # seconds the glow takes to fall, 0–1
- Wled__Devices__0__Mirror=false          # grow from the strip's center
- Wled__Devices__0__Reverse=false         # strip mounted backwards
- Wled__Devices__0__TimeoutSeconds=2</code></pre>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Mode</strong>: Spectrum paints frequencies along the strip,
          Vu is a level meter, Pulse glows the whole strip with the loudness.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Palette</strong> recolors a mode: Classic is each mode's
          traditional look, Solid uses your color for everything, Rainbow
          drifts a full rainbow along the strip.
        </li>
        <li>
          All of it — mode, palette, color, brightness, decay, mirror, reverse
          — is live-tunable from the web UI's Lights card while the music
          plays. These values are just the boot state.
        </li>
        <li>
          When the music stops, the strip goes back to its own WLED effect
          after <strong>TimeoutSeconds</strong>.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="latency">
      <h2 id="latency">One latency for the whole house</h2>
      <pre is:raw><code>- STREAMSEMBLE_GROUP_LATENCY=2.0          # seconds, useful range 1–2</code></pre>
      <p>
        Every speaker plays the sample that is "now" at
        <em>now&nbsp;+&nbsp;this</em>. It's one value for the whole house on
        purpose — it's the headroom that keeps everyone together, not a knob
        for fixing sync (sync is automatic). Below about a second, speakers may
        not have enough runway to start; the hub warns if you leave the useful
        range.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="pin">
      <h2 id="pin">TVs that ask for a PIN</h2>
      <p>
        Some receivers — TVs especially — only play for senders they've been
        formally introduced to. Turn on pairing, start the hub, and the TV
        puts a PIN on screen. Hand it over:
      </p>
      <span class="codelabel">terminal, while the PIN is on screen</span>
      <pre is:raw><code>docker compose exec streamsemble sh -c 'echo 1234-5678 > /data/.streamsemble/pin.txt'</code></pre>
      <p>
        with <code>STREAMSEMBLE_PAIR=1</code> set in the environment. The
        identity is saved in <code>/data</code>, so you pair once — another
        reason to keep that volume.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="spotify">
      <h2 id="spotify">Spotify options</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><code>Spotify__Enabled=false</code> — run as a pure AirPlay hub.</li>
        <li><code>Spotify__Bitrate</code> — stream quality, if you want to set it explicitly.</li>
        <li>
          <code>Spotify__ExtraArgs</code> — passed straight through to
          librespot, for example <code>--volume-ctrl fixed</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Building the image with <code>--build-arg LIBRESPOT_VARIANT=none</code>
          skips compiling librespot entirely (pair it with
          <code>Spotify__Enabled=false</code>).
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="network">
      <h2 id="network">Networking notes</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Host networking is required</strong> in Docker. Speaker
          discovery is multicast and the audio channels are UDP ports the
          speakers dial directly; behind a bridge network, nothing finds the
          hub and no audio arrives.
        </li>
        <li>
          UDP ports <strong>319 and 320</strong> must be free on the box — the
          hub is the house clock and it lives there. If you run nqptp or
          shairport-sync on the same machine, they'll fight for them.
        </li>
        <li>
          Keep the <code>/data</code> volume across restarts: it holds TV
          pairings and Spotify credentials.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <p class="next">
      <a href={`${base}quick-start/`}>Back to the quick start →</a>
    </p>
  </main>
</Base>
